## Decisions

### Aligner uses opus model
The aligner compares semantic intent across multiple spec levels — "does this child's desired outcome #3 contradict the root's undesired outcome #2?" This requires reasoning about meaning, not pattern matching. Opus is the right model for intent-level judgment. This matches the spec-writer and domain-expert (both opus) which also reason about intent semantics.

### Aligner gets no brain/playbook access
The aligner reads specs and compares outcomes. It does not need coding conventions (playbook) or domain research (brain). Keeping its tool surface minimal reduces context burn and keeps it focused on its single job: spec-vs-spec comparison. It uses only `aide_discover` and `aide_read`.

### Color assignment: green
All existing agent colors are taken (red, pink, orange, yellow, purple x2, cyan). Green is the remaining distinct color and semantically fits — green for "aligned/verified."

### Misalignment tag is always `spec-gap`
When the aligner produces `todo.aide`, the `misalignment` value is always `spec-gap` because alignment issues are definitionally spec-level problems — the spec itself contradicts an ancestor. This is distinct from QA's use of `implementation-drift`, `plan-gap`, etc.

### Alignment section placed after Refactor in orchestrator
Alignment is a standalone operation like Refactor — not part of the linear spec-research-plan-build-qa pipeline. It can be run at any time. Placing it after Refactor groups the non-pipeline operations together.

### Tree-walking removal scope
Four agents have explicit "Walk the intent tree" numbered steps that get fully removed. Two agents (spec-writer, auditor) have lighter discover references that are either removed entirely (spec-writer) or simplified (auditor) because their jobs don't require tree-walking. The researcher has no discover references and is untouched. This matches the spec's intent: agents that need discover call it because it is a tool available to them, not because their instructions tell them to.

### Methodology doc created first (Step 1)
The doc is referenced by the agent definition and the index. Creating it first means subsequent steps can reference it without forward-dependency concerns.
